"""Ask the firmware what a stick-curve byte means, by writing one and watching.

Reading the decompiled SDK cannot settle these questions, because the question
is never what Flydigi's app *writes* -- it is what the *pad* does with the byte.
This asks the pad, using the one observable that needs no reference input:
write a value that should silence the stick, and see whether it does.

**What this already established** (all five runs cross-checked against a
baseline, with button events proving somebody was working the pad):

    flat bank at 790+1..9      stick goes COMPLETELY silent      -> LIVE
    isRound at 790+10 = 1      diagonal clamped 1.19 -> 1.00     -> LIVE
    flat core polyline at 109  no effect, even with type=Custom  -> inert
    edge byte at 801 = 236     no effect                         -> inert
    edge byte at 801 = 90      no effect                         -> inert
    edge byte at 801 = 100     no effect, and it is degenerate   -> inert

So the pad plays **the nine-point bank and isRound, and nothing else in either
block**. The core polyline, the type byte, center and edge are not leftovers --
they are the SOURCE the bank is compiled from, host-side: center and edge
position a four-node polyline's endpoints, p1/p2 are its interior nodes, and the
renderer samples it at nine points to get the bank. See PROGRESS.md for the
sampler. What the app cannot tell you, and these runs can, is which of the two
blocks the firmware reads -- Space Station writes both unconditionally. Same architecture as the lighting
config, where the pad has no animation generator and simply plays stored frames.
isRound is the exception because the bank *cannot* express it -- circularity is
two-dimensional and the bank is a magnitude curve -- so the pad must do it.

The consequence for anything built on top: a UI must **compute the bank** from
whatever curve it offers. Writing center, edge or the polyline alone moves a
slider and changes nothing the hand can feel.

Use DEGENERATE values, not nudges. "Does a dead zone of 40 do anything" is
unanswerable without a reference input, and an internal recalibration stage --
this pad has one, and command 3 reports it enabled -- could mask a nudge
entirely. Zeroing a curve or collapsing it to a step cannot be masked: nothing
rescales a constant zero into a smooth sweep.

**Buttons are the liveness signal.** A stick nobody touched and a stick the pad
has silenced produce identical evdev traces -- nothing. Two runs were wasted
before that was obvious. Every run now starts on a button press and counts
button events throughout, so "silent" is a result rather than an empty room.

Nothing is ever committed to flash -- there is no call to `save_config` here at
all. The profile is restored before exit, and even if this is killed mid-run the
pad discards an applied-but-unsaved config the next time it sleeps.

    tools/joystick-curve-probe --baseline            # unmodified, for comparison
    tools/joystick-curve-probe --flat-bank           # the control that found it
    tools/joystick-curve-probe --flat-core --custom-type
"""
import argparse
import os
import select
import sys
import time

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))))

from flydigi import device, evdev, identity, mapping

# Below this the reading is centre noise rather than a deflection the user meant.
NOISE_FLOOR = 0.02


def sweep(seconds, side, arm=True, patience=90.0):
    """Wait for the user to start, then collect |deflection| for one stick.

    **Buttons are the liveness signal, and they are the whole reason this is not
    a plain timed read.** The most interesting outcome of this probe is the
    stick going completely silent -- but a stick nobody touched looks exactly
    the same, and two runs were wasted discovering that. Pressing a button is an
    input the curve cannot suppress, so button events during the window prove
    somebody was working the pad. Zero axis events *alongside* live button
    events is then a real result rather than an empty run.

    So the run starts on a button press rather than on a countdown, which also
    means it cannot begin before the hand is ready.
    """
    path, name = evdev.find_device(vendor=device.VID)
    if path is None:
        sys.exit("no Flydigi gamepad on evdev -- press a button to wake the pad")
    codes = ((evdev.ABS_X, evdev.ABS_Y) if side == "left"
             else (evdev.ABS_RX, evdev.ABS_RY))

    samples = []
    presses = 0
    # Circularity only shows in the corners, and only *unclamped*. A full
    # diagonal is (1.0, 1.0) on a square output -- magnitude 1.41 -- and
    # (0.71, 0.71) on a round one -- magnitude 1.0. Clamping the magnitude to 1,
    # which the analysis below wants, makes those two identical, so the raw
    # extremes are tracked separately.
    extremes = {"magnitude": 0.0, "x": 0.0, "y": 0.0}
    with evdev.Reader(path) as reader:
        # Drop whatever happened before we were watching.
        while select.select([reader], [], [], 0)[0]:
            reader.read()
        pressed = {c for c, v in reader.keys.items() if v}

        def poll(timeout):
            nonlocal pressed, presses
            if not select.select([reader], [], [], timeout)[0]:
                return False
            synced = reader.read()
            now = {c for c, v in reader.keys.items() if v}
            presses += len(now ^ pressed)
            pressed = now
            return synced

        if arm:
            print(f"{name} ({path}): PRESS ANY BUTTON to start the {seconds:.0f}s "
                  "window, then sweep the stick and keep tapping a button")
            giveup = time.time() + patience
            while presses == 0 and time.time() < giveup:
                poll(0.2)
            if presses == 0:
                sys.exit("nothing was pressed -- the pad may have slept")
            print("started")

        deadline = time.time() + seconds
        while time.time() < deadline:
            if not poll(0.2):
                continue
            x = reader.axis(codes[0], -1.0, 1.0)
            y = reader.axis(codes[1], -1.0, 1.0)
            magnitude = (x * x + y * y) ** 0.5
            extremes["magnitude"] = max(extremes["magnitude"], magnitude)
            extremes["x"] = max(extremes["x"], abs(x))
            extremes["y"] = max(extremes["y"], abs(y))
            samples.append(min(1.0, magnitude))
    print("window closed -- you can stop")
    return samples, presses, extremes


def report(samples, presses, extremes):
    moved = [s for s in samples if s > NOISE_FLOOR]
    print(f"\n{len(samples)} axis samples ({len(moved)} a real deflection), "
          f"{presses} button events")
    if not moved:
        # evdev is event-driven, so no axis event means the axis never moved.
        # The buttons say whether that is because the pad suppressed it or
        # because nobody was touching the thing.
        if presses > 1:
            print("SILENT: the pad was being worked -- button events prove it --"
                  " and the stick produced nothing. The block took effect.")
        else:
            print("VOID: no stick and no buttons either, so nobody was at the "
                  "pad. Run it again.")
        return
    if len(moved) < 50:
        print("not enough movement to judge -- run it again and sweep more")
        return
    print(f"reached {max(moved) * 100:.0f}% of full scale at most")

    # The corner reading, and the whole circularity test. A square output region
    # puts a full diagonal at (1.0, 1.0), magnitude 1.41; a round one holds it to
    # magnitude 1.0, i.e. about 0.71 per axis -- which is what makes a game with
    # a per-axis run threshold stop running on the diagonal.
    print(f"furthest corner: magnitude {extremes['magnitude']:.2f} "
          f"(|x| {extremes['x']:.2f}, |y| {extremes['y']:.2f})")
    # 1.05, not the textbook 1.41. A perfect square output region would put the
    # diagonal at 1.41, but the stick's own gate is octagonal and never lets it
    # get there -- measured, Rectangle tops out around 1.19. What separates the
    # two modes is simply whether the diagonal is allowed past the unit circle
    # at all, and Circular pins it to 1.00 exactly.
    if extremes["x"] < 0.9 or extremes["y"] < 0.9:
        print("  -> inconclusive; push the stick right into the diagonal corners")
    elif extremes["magnitude"] >= 1.05:
        print("  -> SQUARE: the diagonal is allowed past the unit circle")
    else:
        print("  -> ROUND: each axis reaches full alone, but the diagonal is "
              "clamped to 1.00 -- about 0.71 per axis, which is what makes a "
              "game with a per-axis run threshold stop on the diagonal")
    # How much of the sweep was spent pinned at the top. A big outer dead zone
    # saturates early, so the stick sits at full scale for most of its travel --
    # which is how a positive `edge` shows itself, since it never lowers the max.
    pinned = sum(1 for s in moved if s >= 0.95) / len(moved)
    print(f"{pinned * 100:.0f}% of the sweep was pinned at full scale")

    # The whole question, in one number: how small can the output get without
    # being zero? A dead zone leaves that near the noise floor; an Offset cannot
    # go below the offset itself.
    floor = min(moved)
    print(f"smallest real deflection seen: {floor * 100:.1f}%")

    print("\nwhere the low end of the sweep landed:")
    for lo in range(0, 40, 4):
        hi = lo + 4
        n = sum(1 for s in moved if lo / 100 <= s < hi / 100)
        print(f"  {lo:2d}-{hi:2d}%  {'#' * min(60, n)}{'' if n else '  (empty)'}")

    print()
    if floor < 0.08:
        print("VERDICT: no gap -- the pad read this byte as a positive dead zone "
              "(or as no adjustment at all).")
    else:
        print(f"VERDICT: a gap of ~{floor * 100:.0f}% -- the pad read this byte "
              "as NEGATIVE, i.e. an Offset of about that much. Compare the gap "
              "against the value you were trying to encode to see which decode "
              "the firmware uses.")


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__,
                                 formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    ap.add_argument("--raw-center", type=int,
                    help="byte to write into the stick's centre field, verbatim")
    ap.add_argument("--raw-edge", type=int,
                    help="byte to write into the extra block's edge field. Read "
                         "against the hard stop rather than the low end: a "
                         "negative edge lowers what FULL travel reports, and "
                         "full travel is the one position a hand can repeat")
    ap.add_argument("--flat-bank", action="store_true",
                    help="set the 9-point bank to all 50, which is no output at "
                         "any deflection. A control: if the stick still reads "
                         "100%% at the stop, the pad is not playing this block "
                         "and no reading taken from it means anything")
    ap.add_argument("--circular", action="store_true",
                    help="set isRound to Circular. Push the stick into the "
                         "DIAGONAL CORNERS: a square output reaches magnitude "
                         "1.41 there, a round one is held to 1.0")
    ap.add_argument("--flat-core", action="store_true",
                    help="flatten the CORE polyline at 109 to zero output, the "
                         "exact analogue of --flat-bank. If the stick still "
                         "works, the pad is not playing the core block")
    ap.add_argument("--custom-type", action="store_true",
                    help="also set the curve type to Custom, in case the "
                         "firmware ignores the fields unless asked to")
    ap.add_argument("--baseline", action="store_true",
                    help="change nothing; just measure the pad as it is")
    ap.add_argument("--side", choices=("left", "right"), default="left")
    ap.add_argument("--seconds", type=float, default=12.0)
    args = ap.parse_args()

    edits = [args.raw_center is not None, args.raw_edge is not None,
             args.flat_bank, args.flat_core, args.custom_type, args.circular]
    if args.baseline and any(edits):
        ap.error("--baseline changes nothing, so it goes on its own")
    if not args.baseline and not any(edits):
        ap.error("pass --baseline, or one of --raw-center / --raw-edge / "
                 "--flat-bank / --flat-core / --custom-type / --circular")

    if args.baseline:
        report(*sweep(args.seconds, args.side))
        return 0

    ctrl = device.Controller()
    # This probe writes a profile blob, and a bare Controller() is whichever
    # Flydigi pad answered first.
    try:
        identity.require(ctrl)
    except identity.WrongDevice as exc:
        sys.exit(f"error: {exc}")
    status = mapping.read_status(ctrl)
    if status is None:
        sys.exit("the pad did not answer -- press a button to wake it")
    cfg_id = status["active"]

    # Edit whichever profile is running, so the change takes effect where the
    # stick is actually being read from.
    original = mapping.read_config(ctrl, cfg_id)
    edited = original.copy()
    index = mapping.SIDES.index(args.side)
    core = mapping.OFF_JOYSTICK_CURVE + index * mapping.CURVE_ENTRY
    extra = mapping.OFF_JOYSTICK_EXTRA + index * mapping.JOYSTICK_EXTRA_ENTRY

    changes = []
    if args.raw_center is not None:
        changes.append(f"centre {edited.blob[core + 1]} -> {args.raw_center & 0xFF}")
        edited.blob[core + 1] = args.raw_center & 0xFF
    if args.raw_edge is not None:
        changes.append(f"edge {edited.blob[extra + 11]} -> {args.raw_edge & 0xFF}")
        edited.blob[extra + 11] = args.raw_edge & 0xFF
    if args.flat_bank:
        changes.append("bank -> all 50 (no output at any deflection)")
        edited.set_joystick_shape(args.side, bank=[50] * mapping.BANK_POINTS)
    if args.circular:
        changes.append("isRound -> Circular")
        edited.set_joystick_shape(args.side, circular=True)
    if args.flat_core:
        changes.append("core polyline -> flat at zero")
        edited.set_joystick_curve(args.side, point1=(0, 0), point2=(127, 0))
    if args.custom_type:
        changes.append("type -> Custom")
        edited.set_joystick_curve(args.side, curve_type=mapping.CURVE_CUSTOM)

    print(f"profile {cfg_id + 1}, {args.side} stick: " + "; ".join(changes)
          + " (applied only, never saved)")
    if args.circular:
        print("push the stick into the DIAGONAL CORNERS -- that is the only "
              "place circularity shows")
    if args.raw_edge is not None or args.flat_bank:
        print("push the stick ALL THE WAY to the stop and hold it there for "
              "part of the run -- the max is the reading that matters")

    try:
        mapping.write_config(ctrl, cfg_id, edited, old=original)
        time.sleep(0.3)
        report(*sweep(args.seconds, args.side))
    finally:
        # Put it back whatever happened. The pad would drop an unsaved config on
        # its next sleep anyway, but leaving someone's stick miscalibrated until
        # then is not a reasonable thing for a probe to do.
        mapping.write_config(ctrl, cfg_id, original, old=edited)
        print(f"\nrestored profile {cfg_id + 1} -- undid: " + "; ".join(changes))
        ctrl.close()
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
